D={
"Seven Years' War":"From 1756 to 1763 Britain and Prussia fought France, Austria and Russia in Europe, North America, the Caribbean and India — often called the first world war. In India, Clive's victory at Plassey and Eyre Coote's at Wandiwash (1760) ended French ambitions. Britain gained Canada and dominance in India, but its war debts led to taxes on American colonies.",
"Black Hole of Calcutta":"After Siraj ud-Daulah captured Fort William in June 1756, British prisoners were held overnight in a small guardroom. John Holwell claimed 123 of 146 died of suffocation; historians think the numbers were exaggerated. The story was used to justify the British counter-attack leading to Plassey.",
"Treaty of Allahabad":"Signed in August 1765 after Buxar, it gave the East India Company the diwani — the right to collect revenue — of Bengal, Bihar and Odisha from Mughal emperor Shah Alam II, in return for an annual tribute. This 'dual government' made the Company a territorial power.",
"Watt's steam engine":"James Watt added a separate condenser to Newcomen's steam engine in 1769, greatly improving efficiency; with Matthew Boulton he built rotary engines to power mills. Steam power drove the Industrial Revolution's factories, railways and steamships.",
"Permanent Settlement":"In 1793 Lord Cornwallis fixed Bengal's land revenue permanently with zamindars as owners. Revenue was high and auctions of defaulting estates were common; peasants lost customary rights, creating a class of absentee landlords.",
"Fall of Srirangapatna":"In May 1799 British forces stormed Tipu Sultan's capital in the Fourth Anglo-Mysore War. Tipu died fighting at the walls. The British restored the Wodeyars as subordinate rulers and took Tipu's rockets to study.",
"Battle of Waterloo":"On 18 June 1815 Napoleon, back from exile, was defeated by Wellington's coalition army and Blücher's Prussians in Belgium. He was exiled to St Helena, where he died in 1821. The Congress of Vienna reshaped Europe.",
"Latin American independence":"Napoleon's invasion of Spain (1808) set off independence movements. Simón Bolívar and José de San Martín led armies that liberated most of Spanish South America by 1825; Mexico won independence in 1821 and Brazil in 1822. New republics faced inequality and military rule.",
"Abolition of sati":"After a long campaign by Raja Ram Mohan Roy, Governor-General William Bentinck outlawed sati, the burning of widows, in Bengal in 1829. Orthodox petitioners appealed to the Privy Council and lost.",
"Macaulay's Minute on Education":"In 1835 Thomas Macaulay argued for English education to create 'a class of persons Indian in blood and colour but English in tastes'. The English Education Act followed, shaping a new Indian elite who later led the national movement.",
"Irish Great Famine":"Potato blight from 1845 destroyed Ireland's staple crop; while food was exported, about 1 million died and 1 million emigrated. Ireland's population has still not recovered to its pre-famine level.",
"Revolutions of 1848":"Revolts spread across France, the German states, Austria and Italy, demanding constitutions and national unity. Most were crushed, but serfdom ended in Austria. That year Marx and Engels published The Communist Manifesto.",
"First Indian railway":"On 16 April 1853 the first passenger train ran 34 km from Bombay to Thane. Railways spread across India, aiding trade and troop movements, and later helped unite the national movement.",
"Taiping Rebellion":"Hong Xiuquan, who believed he was Jesus's brother, led the Taiping Heavenly Kingdom against the Qing (1850–64). Perhaps 20–30 million died, making it among the deadliest civil wars.",
"Meiji Restoration":"In 1868 samurai overthrew the Tokugawa shogunate and restored imperial rule under Emperor Meiji. Japan abolished the samurai class, built railways, a modern army and industry, and became a great power within decades.",
"Suez Canal opens":"The 164 km canal, built by Ferdinand de Lesseps's company with Egyptian forced labour, opened in 1869. It shortened the sea route from Britain to India by about 7,000 km; Britain later seized Egypt (1882).",
"German unification":"Prussia's chancellor Otto von Bismarck won wars against Denmark (1864), Austria (1866) and France (1870–71). In January 1871 William I was proclaimed German Emperor at Versailles.",
"Berlin Conference":"In 1884–85, 14 European nations and the US met in Berlin to set rules for claiming Africa. No Africans were present. It accelerated the Scramble for Africa.",
"Battle of Adwa":"On 1 March 1896 Emperor Menelik II's Ethiopian army defeated an invading Italian army, preserving Ethiopia's independence. It became a symbol of African resistance.",
"Wright brothers' flight":"On 17 December 1903 Orville and Wilbur Wright flew the first powered, controlled aeroplane for 12 seconds at Kitty Hawk, North Carolina.",
"Russo-Japanese War":"In 1904–05 Japan defeated Russia in Manchuria and at Tsushima, the first Asian victory over a European power in modern times. It inspired Indian and Asian nationalists.",
"Xinhai Revolution":"An uprising at Wuchang in October 1911 spread, and the last Qing emperor Puyi abdicated in 1912. Sun Yat-sen became provisional president.",
"Champaran Satyagraha":"In 1917 Gandhi investigated the plight of indigo farmers in Champaran, Bihar, forced to grow indigo for European planters. His non-violent campaign won concessions — his first satyagraha in India.",
"Turkish Republic founded":"After the Ottoman collapse, Mustafa Kemal (Atatürk) won the War of Independence and proclaimed the Republic on 29 October 1923, abolishing the caliphate in 1924.",
"Chinese Civil War":"From 1927 the Nationalists and Communists fought; Mao's Long March (1934–35) saved the Communists. They won in 1949 and the Nationalists fled to Taiwan.",
"Spanish Civil War":"From 1936 to 1939 Franco's Nationalists, aided by Hitler and Mussolini, defeated the Republic. Picasso painted Guernica after its bombing.",
"Battle of Stalingrad":"From August 1942 to February 1943, the Soviets surrounded and destroyed Germany's Sixth Army; about 2 million were killed or wounded. It turned the war in the east.",
"Battles of Imphal and Kohima":"In 1944 British and Indian troops defeated Japan's invasion of northeast India, with the INA fighting on the Japanese side.",
"United Nations founded":"After World War II, 50 nations signed the UN Charter in San Francisco in 1945 to keep peace and promote human rights.",
"Indonesian independence":"Sukarno declared independence on 17 August 1945; after war with the Dutch, it was recognised in 1949.",
"Creation of Israel and the Nakba":"Israel declared independence in May 1948; in the war that followed, about 700,000 Palestinians fled or were expelled — the Nakba.",
"Assassination of Gandhi":"On 30 January 1948 Nathuram Godse shot Gandhi in Delhi; Nehru said 'the light has gone out of our lives'.",
"Korean War":"North Korea invaded the South in 1950; US-led UN forces and China fought until a 1953 armistice. The peninsula remains divided.",
"Ghana's independence":"On 6 March 1957 Ghana under Kwame Nkrumah became the first sub-Saharan African colony to win independence.",
"Sino-Indian War":"In October–November 1962 China defeated India in Aksai Chin and Arunachal Pradesh, then withdrew in the east.",
"Cuban Missile Crisis":"In October 1962 the US discovered Soviet nuclear missiles in Cuba; after 13 days, the USSR withdrew them.",
"Green Revolution in India":"From the mid-1960s high-yield wheat and rice, irrigation and fertilisers made India self-sufficient in food, led by M. S. Swaminathan.",
"Emergency in India":"From 1975 to 1977 Indira Gandhi suspended civil liberties; elections in 1977 ended it.",
"Iranian Revolution":"In 1979 mass protests overthrew the Shah, and Ayatollah Khomeini established an Islamic Republic.",
"Bhopal gas tragedy":"In December 1984 a Union Carbide plant leaked methyl isocyanate, killing thousands.",
"Chernobyl disaster":"In April 1986 a Soviet reactor exploded, spreading radiation across Europe.",
"End of apartheid":"Negotiations led to Mandela's release (1990) and democratic elections (1994).",
"Rwandan genocide":"In 1994 Hutu extremists killed about 800,000 Tutsi and moderate Hutu in 100 days.",
"2008 financial crisis":"The collapse of the US housing bubble and Lehman Brothers triggered a global recession.",
"Arab Spring":"From 2010 protests toppled rulers in Tunisia, Egypt, Libya and Yemen; Syria descended into civil war.",
"India becomes most populous country":"In 2023 India passed China as the world's most populous country, with about 1.43 billion people.",
}
